latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Find the start of the pattern (first non-zero element, typically in the top-left corner).\n   b. Determine the size of the pattern element by examining adjacent non-zero elements.\n   c. Calculate the step size for moving along the diagonal (equal to the pattern element size).\n\n2. Create a new empty grid of the same size as the input grid.\n\n3. Implement the rotation process:\n   a. Start from the identified pattern start position.\n   b. For each pattern element along the diagonal:\n      - Extract the current pattern element.\n      - Rotate the pattern element 90 degrees clockwise.\n      - Calculate the new position for the rotated element (moving from top-left to bottom-left diagonal).\n      - Place the rotated element in the new grid at the calculated position.\n   c. Move to the next pattern element along the diagonal using the calculated step size.\n\n4. Continue the rotation process until reaching the end of the diagonal (bottom-right corner of the grid).\n\n5. Return the new grid with the rotated pattern.\n\nDetailed steps for implementation:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   - Iterate through the grid to find the first non-zero element (start_row, start_col).\n   - From this starting point, determine the width and height of the pattern element by checking adjacent non-zero elements.\n   - Set the step_size to be equal to the width of the pattern element.\n\n2. Create a new ColoredGrid object with the same dimensions as the input grid, initialized with zeros.\n\n3. Implement the rotation process:\n   - Initialize current_row and current_col to start_row and start_col.\n   - Initialize new_row to the last row of the grid and new_col to start_col.\n   - While current_row and current_col are within grid bounds:\n     a. Extract the current pattern element (subgrid of size width x height).\n     b. Rotate this subgrid 90 degrees clockwise.\n     c. Place the rotated subgrid in the new grid at position (new_row - height + 1, new_col).\n     d. Update positions:\n        - current_row += step_size\n        - current_col += step_size\n        - new_row -= step_size\n        - new_col += step_size\n\n4. Repeat step 3 until reaching the end of the diagonal.\n\n5. Return the new grid.\n\nThis plan takes into account the variable size of pattern elements and should work for all the given test cases, as well as potential variations not shown in the examples. It maintains the core concept of rotating the diagonal pattern 90 degrees clockwise while being flexible enough to handle different pattern sizes and grid dimensions."
